{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His early life involved captivity following the Battle of Inab where he spent roughly ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo before gaining freedom through a ransom payment. This long imprisonment shaped his worldview and made him deeply skeptical of reckless military adventures against Muslim forces. He later became one of the most powerful barons in the Latin East and often acted as a regent or advisor to kings who lacked experience or were too young to rule alone.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond inherited his title while still a youth yet his actual rule began only after his release from Syrian captivity. The years he spent in prison allowed him to learn Arabic and understand Muslim culture in ways that few other Crusader nobles ever achieved. This unique perspective influenced his later political strategies and his preference for diplomacy over constant warfare. His ability to negotiate with Muslim leaders set him apart from many of his contemporaries who favored aggressive expansion without regard for consequences.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nHe served as regent for Baldwin IV when the king was still a minor and later during the periods when leprosy rendered the monarch unable to govern effectively. Raymond frequently clashed with the faction led by Agnes of Courtenay and her supporters because he advocated for peace treaties with Saladin while they pushed for confrontation. His political maneuvering aimed to preserve the kingdom through stability rather than through glorious but dangerous battles that could drain resources and manpower. Tensions reached a peak when he opposed the marriage of Sibylla to Guy of Lusignan since he feared Guy would lead the realm to ruin.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nThe final chapter of his life saw him withdraw from court politics after failing to prevent the rise of Guy of Lusignan to the throne. He retired to Tripoli where he focused on administering his own county rather than intervening in royal disputes at Jerusalem. His death occurred shortly before the disastrous Battle of Hattin where the very policies he warned against led to the destruction of the Crusader army. Historians often view him as a pragmatic statesman who understood the limitations of Crusader power better than his rivals did. His cautious approach might have saved the kingdom if his advice had been heeded by the ruling elite during those critical years before the fall of Jerusalem.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.00013710008}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0007010771}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0019687111}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.01948398}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -2.2173173e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.7881409e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.02656e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0025652142}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.09936438}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.004561141}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0.019488174}, {"token": 